From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Sargison <sarol263@student.otago.ac.nz>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 09:09:52 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 37/84] Plutonium: find in page for tabs

EXO-TAB-FEATURES (docs/features/TAB_FEATURES.md, "Find in page"): the
registry's tabs get Chrome's FindTabHelper (FindBarState::
ConfigureWebContents()), and the shell draws the find bar in place of
FindBarController's views bar, which needs a BrowserView.

- "find" (Ctrl+F) asks the shell to open its bar: a "find" tab request
  {initialText, fromSelection, matchCase}, the selection or else the last
  search. It stays open while the bar is; the bar answers {action: "keep"
  | "clear" | "activate"} as it closes, which stops the search. The shell
  asked gets the keyboard focus from the tab, which its page can't take
  itself, and gives it back if it doesn't take the request. With no shell
  to take it, nothing happens. A bar whose shell goes away leaves no
  highlights; a new page closes the bar, as in Chrome.
- "find-text" {text, forward, matchCase, select, scroll} searches, or
  moves to the next match of the same text, and resolves with the result
  once every frame answered.
- "find-next" and "find-previous" (F3, Ctrl+G, Shift+F3, Ctrl+Shift+G)
  search again for the last text, or open the bar if there is none.
- "stop-find" {action} stops the search and closes the bar.
- Results are the tab's state "find": {text, matchCase, matches,
  activeMatch, rect (the active match, in the tab's view), final}.
